WEBVTT

1
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.480
Andrzej Kopeć: Hello.

2
00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.709
Andrey Venuto: Hey guys, so, are we waiting on Kevin? Is that right?

3
00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:06.840
Joseph Denman: Think so.

4
00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:07.760
Andrey Venuto: Okay.

5
00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:10.109
Andrey Venuto: Let me ping him.

6
00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.769
Kent Dybvig: I pinged him, I haven't heard back yet.

7
00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:32.780
Andrey Venuto: Well, I think… I think we can start, if we have anything to discuss,

8
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:38.950
Andrey Venuto: I don't have the agenda, but… maybe…

9
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:44.190
Andrey Venuto: I don't know, does anyone have any topics that they want to discuss? Let me start with… from there.

10
00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.929
Andrey Venuto: Iskander, OpenZeppelin, guys, do you have anything that you want to raise?

11
00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:02.359
Iskander - OZ: I was just, I remember the most important thing is just the ECDC, nothing.

12
00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:04.950
Iskander - OZ: Urgent than this.

13
00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:13.290
Iskander - OZ: I mean, there's just another issue, but I think it's the wallet side, not anything related to compact, so…

14
00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:14.720
Andrey Venuto: Okay.

15
00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.730
Andrey Venuto: Well, let's wait maybe a minute or two more.

16
00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:26.920
Andrey Venuto: if Kevin… Doesn't show up, and we have no extra topics, we can just, like, end the meeting here.

17
00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:36.439
Kent Dybvig: Oh, I have… I have an issue that's come up a couple of times in some old tickets that we were…

18
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.549
Kent Dybvig: that we got forwarded to the GitHub repo, which is…

19
00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.149
Kent Dybvig: What we should be doing about…

20
00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:54.499
Kent Dybvig: Circuits that get too large because we have, say, nested loops, and the compiler ends up taking a really long time.

21
00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:57.070
Kent Dybvig: Or apparently hangs.

22
00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:02.579
Kent Dybvig: When it's pretty clear that we're never going to be able to create a circuit out of it.

23
00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:08.079
Kent Dybvig: We have this arbitrary limit on…

24
00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:16.480
Kent Dybvig: I'm not sure what anymore, where the arbitrary limit is, even if it exists anymore. I think… I think our… we have a limit of…

25
00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:26.109
Kent Dybvig: a really large arbitrary limit on the size of a vector, which is… I don't remember… do you remember, Parisa, like, 4 megabytes or something like that?

26
00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.109
Kent Dybvig: But of course, if you just nest loops 10 times, that 4 megabytes becomes a lot more…

27
00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:46.660
Kent Dybvig: And, there are times when it's pretty clear that we're going to exceed the circuit size, or it should be pretty clear that we're going to exceed the reasonable circuit size, and what should we do about that?

28
00:06:48.020 --> 00:07:00.059
Kent Dybvig: Joe suggested what we could do is just spit out a status message every now and then, or possibly we talked about, if you hit the interrupt key in the compiler, it'll tell you what it's working on.

29
00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:03.790
Kent Dybvig: If it's still… if it's still responsive.

30
00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:11.839
Kent Dybvig: But I think it would be nice if we… if we had some notion of…

31
00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:14.930
Kent Dybvig: Of, this is just too big.

32
00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:19.160
Kent Dybvig: If we could have the compiler

33
00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.860
Kent Dybvig: Terminate with a reasonable message and a reasonable amount of time.

34
00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:31.780
Kent Dybvig: when it's producing something that's clearly not gonna… for which we're not gonna be able to create a circuit. Is there any hope that we can do that?

35
00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:42.419
Joseph Denman: Seems like the… I don't know, like, the real question here is whether or not any contract could be written that has a circuit size so large.

36
00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.430
Joseph Denman: that would even be feasible to use. I mean, like, you know, it's…

37
00:07:48.010 --> 00:08:00.789
Joseph Denman: I don't have an example per se, but is there a contract that would take, I don't know, 12 hours, let's say, to compile, that you could ever reasonably hope to create a proof for that would be useful?

38
00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.930
Joseph Denman: I mean, that would be the…

39
00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.130
Joseph Denman: The case that we're cutting off.

40
00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:10.179
Joseph Denman: And I guess there is, like, the 80-20 rule.

41
00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:15.110
Joseph Denman: You know, design your system so that it supports 80% of use cases.

42
00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:31.489
Joseph Denman: Maybe even in… maybe if we did have, like, a set upper bound, then we would have an override flag for that behavior, or something like that, so that you could support the 20% of use cases that just have massive circuits.

43
00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:44.209
Kent Dybvig: Russ, do you have any opinion on this? I know you've… I think you've waited in the past that you don't like having an arbitrary limit.

44
00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:49.310
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, I…

45
00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:55.640
Thomas Kerber: I mean, I think that the problem with an arbitrary limit is it's not clear what it should be to me.

46
00:08:56.010 --> 00:09:02.180
Thomas Kerber: And you can make a case that… Larger circuits are useful.

47
00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:11.119
Thomas Kerber: Like, just as, you know, just as an example, our SRS is based off the Filecoin SRS.

48
00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:20.129
Thomas Kerber: Which goes up to 2 to the 25 parameters. So, that's also interesting, because that's an absolute upper bound of what we can support on-chain.

49
00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:26.360
Thomas Kerber: In that anything larger than that, you flat out will not be able to create proofs for.

50
00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:30.310
Thomas Kerber: However.

51
00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:42.809
Thomas Kerber: It's that… that's not very useful because… or it's not very useful with what we have right now, because we can't estimate the size in terms of constraints of a circuit at the compiler level very easily.

52
00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:44.930
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

53
00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:54.610
Thomas Kerber: But, like, the reason I bring it up is, you know, the reason that this SRS exists is because Filecoin exists, and because they use proofs that are that large.

54
00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:57.850
Thomas Kerber: Now, do we want to support them on midnight?

55
00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.230
Thomas Kerber: Probably not.

56
00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.950
Thomas Kerber: Might someone want to do something crazy enough to need that large proofs?

57
00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:07.180
Thomas Kerber: Maybe.

58
00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.070
Thomas Kerber: Probably not anytime soon.

59
00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:15.659
Kent Dybvig: And I will say also that the compiler,

60
00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:23.239
Kent Dybvig: Can go through a phase where it generates something really large, and then through optimization, smashes it down to almost nothing.

61
00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.979
Kent Dybvig: And I don't know how we would…

62
00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:34.660
Kent Dybvig: You know, we could… we'd have to wait until the optimization phase to make a definitive call, no matter what.

63
00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:38.539
Kent Dybvig: Because a lot of things can collapse.

64
00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:43.929
Kent Dybvig: And by that time, the compiler may have already taken the 12 hours.

65
00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:48.480
Joseph Denman: So, really, it's…

66
00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:48.950
Thomas Kerber: No.

67
00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:50.410
Joseph Denman: a question…

68
00:10:50.810 --> 00:11:10.139
Joseph Denman: of how long you want the compiler to run. It's like, it's not so much a question as to how big you want… I mean, I guess that's a separate question of how big you want the circuit produced to be, but if that's dependent on later optimizations that could occur after you run for your 12 hours.

69
00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:12.080
Joseph Denman: then…

70
00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:25.419
Joseph Denman: you know, that's kind of a moot point. So maybe the question is just, yeah, is do we want an upper bound on how long the compiler would run before it reaches that optimization stage, if we can't predict ahead of time how big a circuit will be?

71
00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:33.009
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, I've never… I never implemented such a thing in Shea Scheme, and I would be hesitant to implement such a thing in…

72
00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:38.500
Kent Dybvig: Here, the only thing I would implement would be a limit based on on…

73
00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.130
Joseph Denman: It's true, but this is a different domain, right?

74
00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:45.379
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, but I don't think it's a different domain in the sense of…

75
00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:53.630
Kent Dybvig: maybe it's just taking forever for it to generate something that's gonna still get optimized down to nothing, and I don't think it's our job to…

76
00:11:54.030 --> 00:12:00.949
Kent Dybvig: to thwart the compiler in those cases. I think it could be our job to thwart the compiler and give a decent error message when

77
00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:06.919
Kent Dybvig: We know we're not going to be able to generate a circuit that's… that's feasible.

78
00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:09.860
Kent Dybvig: So…

79
00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:10.569
Joseph Denman: But cancer…

80
00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:17.270
Kent Dybvig: It's a different… it's a different question, right? And I don't think… I think that, you know, everybody always has the interrupt key, or the kill.

81
00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:23.270
Kent Dybvig: the kill command. So, they can always… they can always terminate the process if they get bored.

82
00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:32.400
Thomas Kerber: It comes to my mind, because you mentioned, Shay's game,

83
00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:38.339
Thomas Kerber: This is actually quite close to, like, the problem with macro expansion.

84
00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:45.559
Thomas Kerber: Because it's essentially the same thing. Like, we are… we are just unrolling everything, and that's part of the issue.

85
00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:51.729
Thomas Kerber: So I'm curious, like, surely you thought about this while designing Shay's scheme as well?

86
00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:56.099
Thomas Kerber: of what do you do with runaway macroexpansion? And I guess

87
00:12:56.440 --> 00:13:01.989
Thomas Kerber: Is it just the conclusion that you came to, okay, people can kill it if they need to?

88
00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:13.539
Kent Dybvig: Oh, yeah, exactly, because the macroexpander is Turing complete. You can write whatever you want to in it. You should be able to write whatever you want to in it, and if it takes, you know, it takes a long time.

89
00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:18.520
Kent Dybvig: And then you have various tracing tools,

90
00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:26.910
Kent Dybvig: you know, you can trace your macros as they get expanded. We don't have so much visibility into it as that in our case.

91
00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:35.409
Kent Dybvig: We could actually, we could actually gather some statistics.

92
00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:45.429
Kent Dybvig: I mentioned in an earlier meeting that we could print some information whenever you hit a keyboard interrupt, and then ask you if you want to continue or quit.

93
00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:59.989
Kent Dybvig: it could be a different signal, but regardless, it could be… could be interesting to say, okay, you hit CTRL-C while this thing's running, and the console is directed to a,

94
00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:05.829
Kent Dybvig: You know, the… you know, we're running on a… we're running on a TTY instead of…

95
00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:10.149
Kent Dybvig: Going to a file, then… We can say,

96
00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:17.409
Kent Dybvig: All right, we're gonna first… first we're gonna print some information. We are working… we are currently in past such and such. We have…

97
00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:24.030
Kent Dybvig: you know, some… Number of million,

98
00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:29.510
Kent Dybvig: you know, of some things, right?

99
00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:34.059
Kent Dybvig: Expressions and sub-expressions, and

100
00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:42.219
Kent Dybvig: You know, we could… we could try to give some information that would give some insight, so that somebody, when they get… when they interrupt, they're not just frustrated that

101
00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:54.820
Kent Dybvig: it died, and they don't really have a clue why. We simply tell them, you know, we inlined, and it… and this has got to be a thousand lines of code, or a hundred million lines of code, or something like that.

102
00:14:58.070 --> 00:15:03.559
Kent Dybvig: And if it reports that it's 10 lines of code and it's still hanging, then they probably have a bug on their hands.

103
00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:11.260
Kent Dybvig: So we could think about something like that.

104
00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:24.110
Kent Dybvig: It's a little more, you know, it's not just the compiler, it's also ZKAR.

105
00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:29.990
Kent Dybvig: The herb prints some status information while it's running, so…

106
00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:37.729
Kent Dybvig: Not my favorite thing for tools to do, but it is doing it, so maybe it's not as important there.

107
00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:51.650
Kent Dybvig: Still, even for that, it would be useful.

108
00:15:51.970 --> 00:16:00.300
Kent Dybvig: if the scheme process that invoked ZKR process to catch the interrupt and report what it handed to ZKIR

109
00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:04.790
Kent Dybvig: If you're wondering why ZKR is taking a long time.

110
00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:07.629
Kent Dybvig: It's because we handed it something huge.

111
00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:17.090
Andrey Venuto: Andy, go ahead.

112
00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:20.580
Andrzej Kopeć: So, for…

113
00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:31.850
Andrzej Kopeć: B… for large circuits, that probably take hours to even compile, and to… can take a long time to prove.

114
00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:37.579
Andrzej Kopeć: Or kind of ideas,

115
00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:41.799
Andrzej Kopeć: Related to… let's prove that the…

116
00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:53.350
Andrzej Kopeć: state of that chain in that particular block, followed the consensus rules from, what I witnessed, tend to be large circuits.

117
00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:58.489
Andrzej Kopeć: With, with expensive, proofs to, to compute.

118
00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:09.430
Joseph Denman: Yeah, I… I mean, there could be…

119
00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:16.370
Joseph Denman: There could be circuits that you would only want to call one time.

120
00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:33.950
Joseph Denman: And it was very important to call it one time. Maybe I'm misunderstanding what you're saying, but, you know, like, even if it takes, say, 12 hours to make a proof, what have you, if it were really important, and you wanted to do it one time, that's still kind of a valid use case, right?

121
00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:38.360
Andrzej Kopeć: Yes, exactly. I mean,

122
00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:43.909
Andrzej Kopeć: Very specific example of such circuit is,

123
00:17:44.100 --> 00:18:00.810
Andrzej Kopeć: Methril, but adopted to not be a set of, ad hoc threshold signatures, but, where the certificates are proven via zero knowledge. And for…

124
00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:09.169
Andrzej Kopeć: Cardano in particular, the… the circuit talk, I don't know…

125
00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:17.940
Andrzej Kopeć: 10 to 12 hours, something along those lines. I mean, there was still room for improvements, when it comes to…

126
00:18:18.190 --> 00:18:23.979
Andrzej Kopeć: Optimization, but still, it was a very large or a very expensive, circuit.

127
00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:32.160
Andrzej Kopeć: And in such case, there is a certain…

128
00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:43.999
Andrzej Kopeć: There is a certain margin up to how much time can be spent on proving it, because of the value to the rest of the community.

129
00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:02.190
Andrey Venuto: Anyone else, any other topics?

130
00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:19.620
Joseph Denman: Well, I don't know if we reached consensus on that particular topic, but Kent, are you leaning a particular way on this?

131
00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:22.260
Joseph Denman: Sounds like there are trade-offs on both sides.

132
00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:27.730
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, I think I'm leaning towards… You know, I think…

133
00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:30.739
Kent Dybvig: where Thomas is, we probably don't…

134
00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:38.499
Kent Dybvig: We probably don't cut it off based on the proof size, but… and we probably don't cut it off based on compile time either.

135
00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:43.090
Kent Dybvig: But we might, we should consider,

136
00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:49.500
Kent Dybvig: Some way for somebody to get some information out of the compiler, either a tracing mode.

137
00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:55.509
Kent Dybvig: It's more than just tracing passes, but actually is giving statistics about

138
00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:58.620
Kent Dybvig: The size of the programs coming out of different passes.

139
00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:08.510
Kent Dybvig: So that if you do kill it and restart it, you can get that information, or we always gather the information, and we… we spit it out when you hit a keyboard interrupt.

140
00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:14.249
Kent Dybvig: Which might be a less frustrating thing if somebody is… is…

141
00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:17.699
Kent Dybvig: already waited for 2 hours. They don't wanna… they don't wanna…

142
00:20:17.850 --> 00:20:20.259
Kent Dybvig: Restart it with the tracing mode on.

143
00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:25.249
Kent Dybvig: And, I'll wait another 2 hours to get that feedback.

144
00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:35.099
Kent Dybvig: So, let's, let's, let's consider these options, and, create an issue out of it, and,

145
00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:41.169
Kent Dybvig: I don't think it's… I don't think it's a super high priority, but it's something we should… should mull over and try to…

146
00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:43.790
Kent Dybvig: Try to come up with something.

147
00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:58.439
Kent Dybvig: I don't have anything else. Does anybody else have anything else?

148
00:21:01.690 --> 00:21:05.929
Kent Dybvig: All right, let's call it, and thanks, everybody, for attending.

149
00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:07.400
Andrey Venuto: Thank you.

150
00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:08.170
Kent Dybvig: Goodbye.

151
00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:09.120
Andrey Venuto: Bye it.

